---
title: Structured Outputs Overview
id: structured-outputs-overview
order: 1
description: "Constrain TanStack AI responses to a JSON Schema so the model returns typed, predictable objects. Pick the journey that matches what you're building — one-shot extraction, a streaming UI, or a multi-turn structured chat."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - structured outputs
  - json schema
  - zod
  - valibot
  - standard schema
  - type-safe llm
  - outputSchema
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Structured outputs constrain model responses to match a JSON Schema you control. You get back a typed object — not a string you have to parse, not a \"mostly-JSON\" blob you have to guess at, not a regex match. The model either returns something that fits your schema or you get a typed error."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You wire this in once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":". The runtime takes care of the rest: it converts your schema to JSON Schema, hands it to the provider's native structured-output API, validates the response, and infers the TypeScript type from your schema definition."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst Person = z.object({ name: z.string(), age: z.number() });\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"John Doe, 30\" }],\n  outputSchema: Person,\n});\n\nperson.name; // string — fully typed, no cast\nperson.age;  // number","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"schema-libraries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema Libraries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI accepts any library that implements "},{"type":"link","href":"https://standardschema.dev/json-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standard JSON Schema"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://zod.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zod"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (v4.2+)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://arktype.io/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ArkType"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://valibot.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valibot"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@valibot/to-json-schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Plain JSON Schema objects (loses TypeScript type inference — see "},{"type":"link","href":"./one-shot#using-plain-json-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-Shot Extraction"}]},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refer to your schema library's docs for field descriptions, refinements, and enums. TanStack AI converts the schema to JSON Schema automatically."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"provider-support","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider Support"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every adapter handles structured output through its provider's native API:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implementation"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"json_schema"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool-based extraction"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Google Gemini"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"responseSchema"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ollama"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"JSON mode with schema"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter / Grok / Groq"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"json_schema"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claude Code / Codex"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native schema flag on the same harness turn ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--json-schema"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--output-schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenCode / Grok Build / ACP compatible"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same-turn prompt-and-parse"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The provider-specific details are handled for you. The same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" call works across all of them. For a coding agent in a sandbox, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Agents"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"anthropic-schema-complexity-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic schema complexity limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic compiles a structured-output schema into a grammar and rejects schemas it considers too large or too complex with a 400 error — typically "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Schema is too complex for compilation"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"output_config.format.schema: Invalid schema: The compiled grammar is too large"},{"type":"text","value":". This affects Claude models directly and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anthropic/*"},{"type":"text","value":" models routed through OpenRouter, even when every other provider accepts the same schema."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you hit one of these errors, simplify the schema. Complexity is driven less by overall size than by constructs that multiply the grammar's branching — common offenders:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":".optional()"},{"type":"text","value":" fields you don't strictly need"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":".catch()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".default()"},{"type":"text","value":" wrappers (they widen the accepted input)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Union types and deeply nested objects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unconstrained optional strings — tighten with enums or formats where possible"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic's exact limits change over time and aren't all published, so we deliberately don't reproduce the numbers here — see Anthropic's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/structured-outputs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"structured outputs documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the current limits and reduction strategies."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"which-page-do-i-read","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which page do I read?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pick the journey that matches what you're building. The guides under Structured Outputs cover separate use cases. Read the one that fits."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to…"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extract one structured object from a single prompt — and consume it server-side (script, endpoint, CLI) or in a browser via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./one-shot","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-Shot Extraction"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a UI that fills in field-by-field as the model streams (progressive form, live card, typewriter preview)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Walk through a Start app that fills a table from streamed rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../tutorials/streaming-structured-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming Structured Table"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let users iterate on a structured object across multiple turns — each turn produces a new typed object and history stays renderable"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Combine structured output with tool calls (agent loop that runs tools first, then returns a typed object)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./with-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With Tools"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ask a coding agent in a sandbox to inspect files, then return a typed object"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Agents"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The streaming and multi-turn paths both build on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":". The \"with tools\" path layers on top of either. Pick the one that describes your shipping shape — start there, follow the cross-links when you need a piece of another story."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Server-side validation is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"path-dependent"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For the non-streaming agentic path ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":"), the engine runs Standard Schema validation inside the finalization step and routes failures through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" (the awaited promise rejects). For the streaming path ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":"), Standard Schema "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"validation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is deliberately deferred to the consumer — consumers read the object from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value.object"},{"type":"text","value":" field (or call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parseWithStandardSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" themselves on the raw text). The schema you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" on the client is used for TypeScript inference and (in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":") for client-side "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parsePartialJSON"},{"type":"text","value":"-based progressive parsing — the typed-object guarantee comes from the server-side path you pick."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"both"}]},{"type":"text","value":" paths the engine normalizes the captured object before it reaches you: to satisfy strict providers, optional fields are widened to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"required"},{"type":"text","value":" + nullable, so the provider returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" for an absent optional. The engine undoes exactly that widening — an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".optional()"},{"type":"text","value":" field that came back "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" reads back as "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"absent"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"T | undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"), while a genuine "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".nullable()"},{"type":"text","value":" field's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"preserved"}]},{"type":"text","value":". So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value.object"},{"type":"text","value":" (streaming) and the awaited result (non-streaming) both carry the un-widened shape your schema describes."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"middleware-integration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware integration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware configured on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" now observes the final structured-output\nprovider call in addition to the agent loop. Chunks from the structured-output\nadapter are attributed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.phase === 'structuredOutput'"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" fires\nexactly once at the end of the entire run."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Path-dependent:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Adapters that natively combine "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" + a schema-\nconstrained final answer in one streaming call do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" issue a separate\nfinalization round-trip. The engine wires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" into the regular\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatStream"},{"type":"text","value":" request and harvests the structured result from the agent\nloop's final-turn text. On this path the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'structuredOutput'"},{"type":"text","value":" middleware\nphase does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" fire — middleware sees the run through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'beforeModel'"},{"type":"text","value":"\n/ "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'modelStream'"},{"type":"text","value":" as usual, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStructuredOutputConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" is not invoked."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native combined providers:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modern OpenAI (Chat Completions + Responses)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic Claude 4.5+"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gemini 3.x"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grok 4.x family"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter, when every routed model is in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OPENROUTER_COMBINED_TOOLS_AND_SCHEMA_MODELS"},{"type":"text","value":" (see the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapters without native combined-mode support"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (Anthropic 4.4-, Gemini\n2.x, Grok 2/3, Groq, Ollama, and OpenRouter models outside that set) keep\nthe legacy finalization path and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'structuredOutput'"},{"type":"text","value":" phase fires as\nbefore."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"observing-structured-output-chunks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observing structured-output chunks"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { span } from \"./span\";\n\nconst tracing: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"tracing\",\n  onChunk(ctx, chunk) {\n    // Fires for chunks from the agent loop AND the final structured-output call\n    // chunk.type narrows inside a conditional — use a discriminant check first:\n    if (\"type\" in chunk) {\n      span.addEvent(\"chunk\", { phase: ctx.phase, type: chunk.type });\n    }\n  },\n};","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"transforming-the-json-schema-before-the-provider-call","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforming the JSON Schema before the provider call"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStructuredOutputConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" hook when you need to mutate the schema:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { sharedDefs } from \"./schema-defs\";\n\nconst injectDefs: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"inject-defs\",\n  onStructuredOutputConfig(_ctx, config) {\n    return {\n      outputSchema: { ...config.outputSchema, $defs: { ...sharedDefs } },\n    };\n  },\n};","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/middleware.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced: Middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full hook\nreference."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"schema-libraries","text":"Schema Libraries","level":2},{"id":"provider-support","text":"Provider Support","level":2},{"id":"anthropic-schema-complexity-limits","text":"Anthropic schema complexity limits","level":3},{"id":"which-page-do-i-read","text":"Which page do I read?","level":2},{"id":"middleware-integration","text":"Middleware integration","level":2},{"id":"observing-structured-output-chunks","text":"Observing structured-output chunks","level":3},{"id":"transforming-the-json-schema-before-the-provider-call","text":"Transforming the JSON Schema before the provider call","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nStructured outputs constrain model responses to match a JSON Schema you control. You get back a typed object — not a string you have to parse, not a \"mostly-JSON\" blob you have to guess at, not a regex match. The model either returns something that fits your schema or you get a typed error.\n\nYou wire this in once with `outputSchema`. The runtime takes care of the rest: it converts your schema to JSON Schema, hands it to the provider's native structured-output API, validates the response, and infers the TypeScript type from your schema definition.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst Person = z.object({ name: z.string(), age: z.number() });\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"John Doe, 30\" }],\n  outputSchema: Person,\n});\n\nperson.name; // string — fully typed, no cast\nperson.age;  // number\n```\n\n## Schema Libraries\n\nTanStack AI accepts any library that implements [Standard JSON Schema](https://standardschema.dev/json-schema):\n\n- [Zod](https://zod.dev/) (v4.2+)\n- [ArkType](https://arktype.io/)\n- [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/) (via `@valibot/to-json-schema`)\n- Plain JSON Schema objects (loses TypeScript type inference — see [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot#using-plain-json-schema))\n\nRefer to your schema library's docs for field descriptions, refinements, and enums. TanStack AI converts the schema to JSON Schema automatically.\n\n## Provider Support\n\nEvery adapter handles structured output through its provider's native API:\n\n| Provider | Implementation |\n|---|---|\n| OpenAI | `response_format` with `json_schema` |\n| Anthropic | Tool-based extraction |\n| Google Gemini | `responseSchema` |\n| Ollama | JSON mode with schema |\n| OpenRouter / Grok / Groq | `response_format` with `json_schema` |\n| Claude Code / Codex | Native schema flag on the same harness turn (`--json-schema` / `--output-schema`) |\n| OpenCode / Grok Build / ACP compatible | Same-turn prompt-and-parse |\n\nThe provider-specific details are handled for you. The same `chat({ outputSchema })` call works across all of them. For a coding agent in a sandbox, see [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n### Anthropic schema complexity limits\n\nAnthropic compiles a structured-output schema into a grammar and rejects schemas it considers too large or too complex with a 400 error — typically `Schema is too complex for compilation` or `output_config.format.schema: Invalid schema: The compiled grammar is too large`. This affects Claude models directly and `anthropic/*` models routed through OpenRouter, even when every other provider accepts the same schema.\n\nIf you hit one of these errors, simplify the schema. Complexity is driven less by overall size than by constructs that multiply the grammar's branching — common offenders:\n\n- `.optional()` fields you don't strictly need\n- `.catch()` / `.default()` wrappers (they widen the accepted input)\n- Union types and deeply nested objects\n- Unconstrained optional strings — tighten with enums or formats where possible\n\nAnthropic's exact limits change over time and aren't all published, so we deliberately don't reproduce the numbers here — see Anthropic's [structured outputs documentation](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/structured-outputs) for the current limits and reduction strategies.\n\n## Which page do I read?\n\nPick the journey that matches what you're building. The guides under Structured Outputs cover separate use cases. Read the one that fits.\n\n| You want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Extract one structured object from a single prompt — and consume it server-side (script, endpoint, CLI) or in a browser via `final` | [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot) |\n| Build a UI that fills in field-by-field as the model streams (progressive form, live card, typewriter preview) | [Streaming UIs](./streaming) |\n| Walk through a Start app that fills a table from streamed rows | [Streaming Structured Table](../tutorials/streaming-structured-table) |\n| Let users iterate on a structured object across multiple turns — each turn produces a new typed object and history stays renderable | [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) |\n| Combine structured output with tool calls (agent loop that runs tools first, then returns a typed object) | [With Tools](./with-tools) |\n| Ask a coding agent in a sandbox to inspect files, then return a typed object | [Harness Agents](./harnesses) |\n\nThe streaming and multi-turn paths both build on `useChat({ outputSchema })`. The \"with tools\" path layers on top of either. Pick the one that describes your shipping shape — start there, follow the cross-links when you need a piece of another story.\n\n> **Note:** Server-side validation is **path-dependent**. For the non-streaming agentic path (`await chat({ outputSchema })`), the engine runs Standard Schema validation inside the finalization step and routes failures through `onError` (the awaited promise rejects). For the streaming path (`chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })`), Standard Schema _validation_ is deliberately deferred to the consumer — consumers read the object from the `structured-output.complete` event's `value.object` field (or call `parseWithStandardSchema` themselves on the raw text). The schema you pass to `useChat({ outputSchema })` on the client is used for TypeScript inference and (in `useChat`) for client-side `parsePartialJSON`-based progressive parsing — the typed-object guarantee comes from the server-side path you pick.\n>\n> On **both** paths the engine normalizes the captured object before it reaches you: to satisfy strict providers, optional fields are widened to `required` + nullable, so the provider returns `null` for an absent optional. The engine undoes exactly that widening — an `.optional()` field that came back `null` reads back as **absent** (matching `T | undefined`), while a genuine `.nullable()` field's `null` is **preserved**. So `value.object` (streaming) and the awaited result (non-streaming) both carry the un-widened shape your schema describes.\n\n## Middleware integration\n\nMiddleware configured on `chat()` now observes the final structured-output\nprovider call in addition to the agent loop. Chunks from the structured-output\nadapter are attributed to `ctx.phase === 'structuredOutput'`; `onFinish` fires\nexactly once at the end of the entire run.\n\n> **Path-dependent:** Adapters that natively combine `tools` + a schema-\n> constrained final answer in one streaming call do **not** issue a separate\n> finalization round-trip. The engine wires `outputSchema` into the regular\n> `chatStream` request and harvests the structured result from the agent\n> loop's final-turn text. On this path the `'structuredOutput'` middleware\n> phase does **not** fire — middleware sees the run through `'beforeModel'`\n> / `'modelStream'` as usual, and `onStructuredOutputConfig` is not invoked.\n>\n> **Native combined providers:**\n> - Modern OpenAI (Chat Completions + Responses)\n> - Anthropic Claude 4.5+\n> - Gemini 3.x\n> - Grok 4.x family\n> - OpenRouter, when every routed model is in\n>   `OPENROUTER_COMBINED_TOOLS_AND_SCHEMA_MODELS` (see the\n>   [OpenRouter adapter](../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together))\n>\n> **Adapters without native combined-mode support** (Anthropic 4.4-, Gemini\n> 2.x, Grok 2/3, Groq, Ollama, and OpenRouter models outside that set) keep\n> the legacy finalization path and the `'structuredOutput'` phase fires as\n> before.\n\n### Observing structured-output chunks\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { span } from \"./span\";\n\nconst tracing: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"tracing\",\n  onChunk(ctx, chunk) {\n    // Fires for chunks from the agent loop AND the final structured-output call\n    // chunk.type narrows inside a conditional — use a discriminant check first:\n    if (\"type\" in chunk) {\n      span.addEvent(\"chunk\", { phase: ctx.phase, type: chunk.type });\n    }\n  },\n};\n```\n\n### Transforming the JSON Schema before the provider call\n\nUse the `onStructuredOutputConfig` hook when you need to mutate the schema:\n\n```ts\nimport type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { sharedDefs } from \"./schema-defs\";\n\nconst injectDefs: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"inject-defs\",\n  onStructuredOutputConfig(_ctx, config) {\n    return {\n      outputSchema: { ...config.outputSchema, $defs: { ...sharedDefs } },\n    };\n  },\n};\n```\n\nSee [Advanced: Middleware](../advanced/middleware.md) for the full hook\nreference.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Structured Outputs Overview\nid: structured-outputs-overview\norder: 1\ndescription: \"Constrain TanStack AI responses to a JSON Schema so the model returns typed, predictable objects. Pick the journey that matches what you're building — one-shot extraction, a streaming UI, or a multi-turn structured chat.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - structured outputs\n  - json schema\n  - zod\n  - valibot\n  - standard schema\n  - type-safe llm\n  - outputSchema\n---\n\nStructured outputs constrain model responses to match a JSON Schema you control. You get back a typed object — not a string you have to parse, not a \"mostly-JSON\" blob you have to guess at, not a regex match. The model either returns something that fits your schema or you get a typed error.\n\nYou wire this in once with `outputSchema`. The runtime takes care of the rest: it converts your schema to JSON Schema, hands it to the provider's native structured-output API, validates the response, and infers the TypeScript type from your schema definition.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst Person = z.object({ name: z.string(), age: z.number() });\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"John Doe, 30\" }],\n  outputSchema: Person,\n});\n\nperson.name; // string — fully typed, no cast\nperson.age;  // number\n```\n\n## Schema Libraries\n\nTanStack AI accepts any library that implements [Standard JSON Schema](https://standardschema.dev/json-schema):\n\n- [Zod](https://zod.dev/) (v4.2+)\n- [ArkType](https://arktype.io/)\n- [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/) (via `@valibot/to-json-schema`)\n- Plain JSON Schema objects (loses TypeScript type inference — see [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot#using-plain-json-schema))\n\nRefer to your schema library's docs for field descriptions, refinements, and enums. TanStack AI converts the schema to JSON Schema automatically.\n\n## Provider Support\n\nEvery adapter handles structured output through its provider's native API:\n\n| Provider | Implementation |\n|---|---|\n| OpenAI | `response_format` with `json_schema` |\n| Anthropic | Tool-based extraction |\n| Google Gemini | `responseSchema` |\n| Ollama | JSON mode with schema |\n| OpenRouter / Grok / Groq | `response_format` with `json_schema` |\n| Claude Code / Codex | Native schema flag on the same harness turn (`--json-schema` / `--output-schema`) |\n| OpenCode / Grok Build / ACP compatible | Same-turn prompt-and-parse |\n\nThe provider-specific details are handled for you. The same `chat({ outputSchema })` call works across all of them. For a coding agent in a sandbox, see [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n### Anthropic schema complexity limits\n\nAnthropic compiles a structured-output schema into a grammar and rejects schemas it considers too large or too complex with a 400 error — typically `Schema is too complex for compilation` or `output_config.format.schema: Invalid schema: The compiled grammar is too large`. This affects Claude models directly and `anthropic/*` models routed through OpenRouter, even when every other provider accepts the same schema.\n\nIf you hit one of these errors, simplify the schema. Complexity is driven less by overall size than by constructs that multiply the grammar's branching — common offenders:\n\n- `.optional()` fields you don't strictly need\n- `.catch()` / `.default()` wrappers (they widen the accepted input)\n- Union types and deeply nested objects\n- Unconstrained optional strings — tighten with enums or formats where possible\n\nAnthropic's exact limits change over time and aren't all published, so we deliberately don't reproduce the numbers here — see Anthropic's [structured outputs documentation](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/structured-outputs) for the current limits and reduction strategies.\n\n## Which page do I read?\n\nPick the journey that matches what you're building. The guides under Structured Outputs cover separate use cases. Read the one that fits.\n\n| You want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Extract one structured object from a single prompt — and consume it server-side (script, endpoint, CLI) or in a browser via `final` | [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot) |\n| Build a UI that fills in field-by-field as the model streams (progressive form, live card, typewriter preview) | [Streaming UIs](./streaming) |\n| Walk through a Start app that fills a table from streamed rows | [Streaming Structured Table](../tutorials/streaming-structured-table) |\n| Let users iterate on a structured object across multiple turns — each turn produces a new typed object and history stays renderable | [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) |\n| Combine structured output with tool calls (agent loop that runs tools first, then returns a typed object) | [With Tools](./with-tools) |\n| Ask a coding agent in a sandbox to inspect files, then return a typed object | [Harness Agents](./harnesses) |\n\nThe streaming and multi-turn paths both build on `useChat({ outputSchema })`. The \"with tools\" path layers on top of either. Pick the one that describes your shipping shape — start there, follow the cross-links when you need a piece of another story.\n\n> **Note:** Server-side validation is **path-dependent**. For the non-streaming agentic path (`await chat({ outputSchema })`), the engine runs Standard Schema validation inside the finalization step and routes failures through `onError` (the awaited promise rejects). For the streaming path (`chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })`), Standard Schema _validation_ is deliberately deferred to the consumer — consumers read the object from the `structured-output.complete` event's `value.object` field (or call `parseWithStandardSchema` themselves on the raw text). The schema you pass to `useChat({ outputSchema })` on the client is used for TypeScript inference and (in `useChat`) for client-side `parsePartialJSON`-based progressive parsing — the typed-object guarantee comes from the server-side path you pick.\n>\n> On **both** paths the engine normalizes the captured object before it reaches you: to satisfy strict providers, optional fields are widened to `required` + nullable, so the provider returns `null` for an absent optional. The engine undoes exactly that widening — an `.optional()` field that came back `null` reads back as **absent** (matching `T | undefined`), while a genuine `.nullable()` field's `null` is **preserved**. So `value.object` (streaming) and the awaited result (non-streaming) both carry the un-widened shape your schema describes.\n\n## Middleware integration\n\nMiddleware configured on `chat()` now observes the final structured-output\nprovider call in addition to the agent loop. Chunks from the structured-output\nadapter are attributed to `ctx.phase === 'structuredOutput'`; `onFinish` fires\nexactly once at the end of the entire run.\n\n> **Path-dependent:** Adapters that natively combine `tools` + a schema-\n> constrained final answer in one streaming call do **not** issue a separate\n> finalization round-trip. The engine wires `outputSchema` into the regular\n> `chatStream` request and harvests the structured result from the agent\n> loop's final-turn text. On this path the `'structuredOutput'` middleware\n> phase does **not** fire — middleware sees the run through `'beforeModel'`\n> / `'modelStream'` as usual, and `onStructuredOutputConfig` is not invoked.\n>\n> **Native combined providers:**\n> - Modern OpenAI (Chat Completions + Responses)\n> - Anthropic Claude 4.5+\n> - Gemini 3.x\n> - Grok 4.x family\n> - OpenRouter, when every routed model is in\n>   `OPENROUTER_COMBINED_TOOLS_AND_SCHEMA_MODELS` (see the\n>   [OpenRouter adapter](../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together))\n>\n> **Adapters without native combined-mode support** (Anthropic 4.4-, Gemini\n> 2.x, Grok 2/3, Groq, Ollama, and OpenRouter models outside that set) keep\n> the legacy finalization path and the `'structuredOutput'` phase fires as\n> before.\n\n### Observing structured-output chunks\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { span } from \"./span\";\n\nconst tracing: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"tracing\",\n  onChunk(ctx, chunk) {\n    // Fires for chunks from the agent loop AND the final structured-output call\n    // chunk.type narrows inside a conditional — use a discriminant check first:\n    if (\"type\" in chunk) {\n      span.addEvent(\"chunk\", { phase: ctx.phase, type: chunk.type });\n    }\n  },\n};\n```\n\n### Transforming the JSON Schema before the provider call\n\nUse the `onStructuredOutputConfig` hook when you need to mutate the schema:\n\n```ts\nimport type { ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { sharedDefs } from \"./schema-defs\";\n\nconst injectDefs: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: \"inject-defs\",\n  onStructuredOutputConfig(_ctx, config) {\n    return {\n      outputSchema: { ...config.outputSchema, $defs: { ...sharedDefs } },\n    };\n  },\n};\n```\n\nSee [Advanced: Middleware](../advanced/middleware.md) for the full hook\nreference.\n"}
